Implemented a root-cause fix in:

`firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/usart_irq/usart_irq.c`

### Root cause
The ISR only handled `RXNE` and `TXE`, but ignored UART error flags (`ORE`, `NE`, `FE`, `PE`).  
On STM32F4, these error conditions can keep the USART interrupt pending unless cleared with the required sequence (read `SR`, then read `DR`). Over time/noisy line conditions can leave IRQ asserted continuously, making the LED appear stuck (it stays in its last toggled state, often ON).

### Fix applied
Inside `usart2_isr()`:

- Snapshot `CR1` and `SR` once at ISR entry.
- Added explicit error handling branch:
  - If any of `ORE|NE|FE|PE` are set, read `DR` to clear error state and return.
- Kept existing RX/TX behavior, but switched comparisons to unsigned-safe literals (`0U`) for deterministic embedded style.

### Why this resolves the issue
Clearing error flags prevents stale USART error interrupts from continuously retriggering the ISR when no valid RX data is present, eliminating the long-run “LED stuck on” failure mode tied to uncleared UART error state.

### Note
A local build attempt was blocked by missing `libopencm3` path/toolchain setup in this environment (`OPENCM3_DIR` not configured), but the source-level fix is complete and targeted to the observed fault mechanism.
